+We conduct experiments on SRA-Bench, which comprises six capability-intensive benchmarks: TheoremQA~\citep{chen-etal-2023-theoremqa}, LogicBench~\citep{parmar-etal-2024-logicbench}, ToolQA~\citep{NEURIPS2023_9cb2a749}, CHAMP~\citep{mao-etal-2024-champ}, MedCalc-Bench~\citep{NEURIPS2024_99e81750}, and BigCodeBench~\citep{ICLR2025_a6a90bcc}. These datasets cover diverse task settings, including mathematical reasoning, formal logic, tool use, and code generation. Each instance is paired with annotated gold skill(s) and evaluated against a shared external skill corpus, enabling decomposed analysis of retrieval, incorporation, and end-task execution. We filter tasks and construct skill-balanced train, validation, and test partitions targeting global proportions of 70\%, 15\%, and 15\%. Every retained gold skill occurs in all three partitions. This design supports evaluating RouterBSI\textquoteright{}s adaptation to held-out tasks involving skills represented during training. Predicate updates require a skill to appear in a training task\textquoteright{}s retrieved top-$k$ pool and incur a routing error; skills never retrieved during training retain their cold-start predicates. These coverage constraints allow individual datasets\textquoteright{} proportions to differ from the global targets; Appendix~\ref{app:data-splits} reports the exact counts.
